Project Journal — Mayur goyal
Project: ReVive — Financial Anomaly Detection (UCS503 Semester Project)

Week 5
Focus: Assembling the full documentation, proposal, and presentation deck

Summary
The final week brought everything together — compiling the completed diagrams, the working demo app, and both other tracks' results and write-ups into a finished documentation set, a course-compliant proposal, and a presentation deck ready for the prototype evaluation.

What I did
●	Assembled the full documentation document: introduction, requirements, architecture (with all five finished diagrams inserted), the detection engine's verified results, the baseline/ensemble comparison and limitations discussion, and a project timeline.
●	Restructured the proposal to match the course's actual expected template (found via a real sample submission in the course's own repository) rather than a generic report structure, while still including the literature review and state-of-the-art sections specifically requested by the professor.
●	Took a final pass through the Streamlit app to confirm it was stable and demo-ready, and prepared a short walkthrough script for presenting it live during the evaluation.
●	Built the presentation deck from the finished documentation: problem statement, approach and novelty, architecture diagrams, results comparison (detection engine vs. classical ensemble), limitations, and a live demo slide.
●	Did a final consistency pass across every deliverable — documentation, proposal, diagrams, demo app, and slides — to make sure numbers, terminology, and the project name were consistent everywhere before submission.

Technical details / observations
●	Kept the presentation's results slide condensed to the key comparison numbers and one interpretive line, saving the fuller methodology discussion (including the caught-and-fixed averaging bug) for the written documentation and for questions during the viva.

Issues / blockers
●	None outstanding — all deliverables were internally consistent with each other after the final review pass.

Learnings
●	Doing a dedicated final consistency pass across every deliverable at the end (rather than assuming each piece was fine in isolation) caught small mismatches that would have been easy to miss otherwise.

Time spent (approx.): ~10 hours (documentation assembly, proposal restructuring, final demo polish, building the presentation deck, final consistency review).
Plan for next week: Rehearse the live demo and presentation with the team, and be ready to walk through the diagrams, the dashboard, and the documentation structure in the viva.
